{"id":1089,"date":"2011-08-12T00:00:00","date_gmt":"2011-08-12T00:00:00","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/imdb.com"},"modified":"2011-08-12T00:00:00","modified_gmt":"2011-08-12T00:00:00","slug":"crazy-stupid-love-2011","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/2011\/08\/12\/crazy-stupid-love-2011\/","title":{"rendered":"Crazy, Stupid, Love. (2011)"},"content":{"rendered":"<h4><i><b>Greg Says: Engaging romantic comedy with a Shakespearian twist<\/b><\/i><\/h4>\n<p>Title: <b>Crazy, Stupid, Love. (2011)<\/b><br \/>\nDate: <b>12 August 2011<\/b><br \/>\nRecommendation: <b>See it in theaters.<\/b><br \/>\nHelpful: <b>0 out of 1 found this helpful.<\/b><\/p>\n<h4>\n<div style='color:red'><b>SPOILERS WITHIN<\/b><\/div style='color:red'><\/h4>\n<p>The film opens with Cal (Steve Carell) being informed by his wife Emily (Julianne Moore) that she wants a divorce. After 25 years of wedded bliss that started in High School, this comes as a great shock to Cal. He starts hanging out at a local bar where lounge lizard, local slickster Jacob (Ryan Gosling) takes pity on our schlemiel and takes him in hand to give him a make-over. Cal then goes on to bed 9 women and turns into a slick dude himself.<\/p>\n<p>If this were any ordinary movie, the rest of the plot would be obvious. It doesn&#8217;t give away too much to say that one of the women Cal conquers turns out to be his son&#8217;s teacher (Marisa Tomei). In fact, the movie plays very much along Hollywood formula lines that the non-Hollywood ending is a complete surprise.<\/p>\n<p>I wish I could tell you more. But to do so would spoil the film for you. Steve Carell is a master of the underplayed comedian. Julianne Moore is lovely at age 50 (although she claims to be 44 in the film \u2013 it is a comedy after all). Emma Stone is smart-sexy. And a special appearance by Kevin Bacon was not unwelcome.<\/p>\n<p>You might &#8220;wait for the instant download&#8221; but I recommend you &#8220;see it in theaters&#8221; because you won&#8217;t want to have the ending spoiled for you around the water cooler.<\/p>\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Greg Says: Engaging romantic comedy with a Shakespearian twist<\/p>\n","protected":false},"author":2,"featured_media":0,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"open","sticky":false,"template":"","format":"standard","meta":{"footnotes":""},"categories":[43],"tags":[],"class_list":["post-1089","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","hentry","category-review"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts\/1089","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/post"}],"author":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/users\/2"}],"replies":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/comments?post=1089"}],"version-history":[{"count":0,"href":"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts\/1089\/revisions"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=1089"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/categories?post=1089"},{"taxonomy":"post_tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/agilewriters.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/tags?post=1089"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}